select energy_manager.load_cards($cards$[
{"id":"p4-001","paper":4,"topic":"Boiler efficiency","kind":"fact","front":"What is the direct-method (input-output) formula for boiler efficiency?","back":"η = [Q × (hg − hf)] / (q × GCV) × 100, where Q = steam flow, hg = steam enthalpy, hf = feed-water enthalpy, q = fuel flow.","explain":"The direct method compares useful heat added to the steam with the heat in the fuel fired, both on the same time basis (e.g. kg/h). Feed-water enthalpy in kcal/kg is roughly equal to its temperature in °C. It is quick but gives no clue as to where the losses occur, which is why the indirect method is preferred for diagnosis.","flash":true,"source":"syllabus","order":4000},
{"id":"p4-002","paper":4,"topic":"Boiler efficiency","kind":"fact","front":"What is the evaporation ratio (ER) of a boiler, and what are typical values?","back":"ER = kg of steam generated per kg of fuel fired. Typical: coal-fired ≈ 6, oil-fired ≈ 13.","explain":"ER links straight to efficiency: η = ER × (hg − hf) / GCV. Because ER uses only measured steam and fuel quantities, it does not depend on whether efficiency is later stated on a GCV or NCV basis (BEE 2025 T/F: True). A falling ER at the same steam conditions is an early sign of falling efficiency.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2025 P4 Q1","order":4001},
{"id":"p4-003","paper":4,"topic":"Boiler efficiency","kind":"fact","front":"If a boiler produces wet steam, does a high evaporation ratio prove high efficiency?","back":"No. Wet steam has a lower enthalpy, so kg of 'steam' per kg of fuel looks high while the useful heat per kg is low.","explain":"Water carried over with the steam is counted in the steam flow, which inflates the ER. Always compare ER together with the steam enthalpy (pressure, temperature, dryness). BEE 2025 asked this as a True/False item (answer: False).","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P4 Q7","order":4002},
{"id":"p4-004","paper":4,"topic":"Boiler efficiency","kind":"fact","front":"Does the direct method of boiler efficiency need the steam generation pressure?","back":"Yes. You need pressure (and temperature for superheated steam) to read the steam enthalpy hg from steam tables.","explain":"The direct method needs steam flow, fuel flow, fuel GCV, feed-water temperature, and steam pressure/temperature. Without pressure you cannot fix hg, so you cannot compute the useful heat. BEE 2023 asked this as a True/False item (answer: False).","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2023 P4 Q10","order":4003},
{"id":"p4-005","paper":4,"topic":"Boiler efficiency","kind":"fact","front":"Which losses are counted in the indirect (heat-loss) method of boiler efficiency, and is blowdown loss among them?","back":"Dry flue gas, H₂ in fuel, moisture in fuel, moisture in air, partial combustion (CO), surface radiation & convection, and unburnt in fly ash & bottom ash. Blowdown loss is NOT included.","explain":"Efficiency = 100 − (sum of losses). The method follows BS 845 / ASME PTC-4-1 practice and is done on a GCV basis. Blowdown is treated as a separate consideration, not as a loss in the standard indirect calculation (BEE 2025 T/F: False). The indirect method's advantage is that it shows where the losses are.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P4 Q5","order":4004},
{"id":"p4-006","paper":4,"topic":"Combustion","kind":"fact","front":"What is the formula for the theoretical (stoichiometric) air needed to burn a fuel, from its ultimate analysis?","back":"Theoretical air (kg/kg fuel) = [11.6 C + 34.8 (H − O/8) + 4.35 S] / 100, with C, H, O, S in % by mass.","explain":"The constants come from the oxygen needed to burn each element, divided by 0.23 (the mass fraction of O₂ in air). The O/8 term takes off hydrogen that is already 'bound' to the fuel's own oxygen. Typical results: about 14 kg/kg for fuel oil, 4–6 kg/kg for coal and biomass.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":4005},
{"id":"p4-007","paper":4,"topic":"Combustion","kind":"fact","front":"How do you estimate excess air from the measured O₂ in flue gas?","back":"Excess air % = O₂% / (21 − O₂%) × 100. Actual air = theoretical air × (1 + EA/100).","explain":"For example, 3% O₂ gives 3/18 = 16.7% excess air and 6% O₂ gives 40%. Too much excess air raises the dry-flue-gas loss. Too little gives CO and unburnt fuel. O₂ is the preferred measurement because it depends less on fuel type than CO₂.","flash":true,"source":"syllabus","order":4006},
{"id":"p4-008","paper":4,"topic":"Boiler efficiency","kind":"fact","front":"Give the formulas for the dry-flue-gas loss and the mass of dry flue gas.","back":"L1 = m_dfg × Cp × (Tf − Ta) / GCV × 100. m_dfg = C×44/12 + S×64/32 + N + 0.77×AAS + 0.23×(AAS − TA)  (or ≈ AAS + 1 − 9H − M).","explain":"C, S, N, H, M are kg per kg of fuel; AAS = actual air supplied and TA = theoretical air. The CO₂, SO₂, nitrogen and unused O₂ make up the dry gas, and water vapour is dealt with separately. Cp of flue gas is usually taken as 0.23–0.29 kcal/kg°C, as the question gives it. This is normally the largest single boiler loss.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":4007},
{"id":"p4-009","paper":4,"topic":"Boiler efficiency","kind":"fact","front":"Give the boiler loss formulas for hydrogen in fuel, moisture in fuel, and moisture in air.","back":"L2 = 9H[584 + Cp_s(Tf − Ta)]/GCV ×100; L3 = M[584 + Cp_s(Tf − Ta)]/GCV ×100; L4 = AAS × humidity × Cp_s × (Tf − Ta)/GCV ×100.","explain":"Each kg of hydrogen forms 9 kg of water. That water, like the fuel's own moisture, takes latent heat (584 kcal/kg) plus superheat out of the stack. Moisture in the combustion air is already vapour, so only its sensible heat counts. Cp_s (superheated vapour) is about 0.43–0.45 kcal/kg°C.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":4008},
{"id":"p4-010","paper":4,"topic":"Boiler efficiency","kind":"fact","front":"What is the formula for the heat loss from partial combustion of carbon to CO?","back":"Loss (kcal/kg fuel) = [%CO × C / (%CO + %CO₂)] × 5654; % loss = that ÷ GCV × 100. (CO in ppm ÷ 10,000 = %.)","explain":"C is kg of carbon per kg of fuel, and 5654 kcal/kg is the heat lost per kg of carbon that burns only to CO. Lower CO in the flue gas means better combustion efficiency (BEE 2024 T/F: True). Example: 5000 ppm = 0.5% CO.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2023 P4 L2","order":4009},
{"id":"p4-011","paper":4,"topic":"Boiler blowdown","kind":"fact","front":"What fraction of high-pressure blowdown flashes to steam in a flash vessel?","back":"Flash fraction = (S1 − S2) / L2, where S1 = sensible heat of the blowdown at boiler pressure, S2 = sensible heat at flash pressure, and L2 = latent heat at flash pressure.","explain":"The extra sensible heat the water holds above saturation at the lower pressure can only go into evaporating part of it. Example: (352 − 120)/526 ≈ 0.44 kg of flash steam per kg of blowdown. The flash steam can feed the deaerator or LP process heating.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2023 P4 N3","order":4010},
{"id":"p4-012","paper":4,"topic":"Boiler blowdown","kind":"fact","front":"What is the formula for the blowdown percentage of a boiler?","back":"Blowdown % = (Feed-water TDS × % make-up water) / (Maximum permissible TDS in boiler water).","explain":"Blowdown keeps dissolved solids in the drum below the limit that causes foaming and carry-over. Too much blowdown wastes heat and treated water. Too little causes scaling and carry-over. Better condensate return lowers feed TDS and so lowers blowdown.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":4011},
{"id":"p4-013","paper":4,"topic":"Boiler efficiency","kind":"problem","front":"A coal-fired boiler generates 120 TPH of steam (enthalpy 790 kcal/kg) from feed water at 130 °C. Coal GCV is 4000 kcal/kg and the measured evaporation ratio is 4.8. Find (a) the boiler efficiency, (b) the coal consumption in TPH, and (c) the coal saved per day if efficiency improves by 2% relative to its present value.","back":"η = 79.2%; coal = 25.0 TPH; new η = 80.78%; saving ≈ 0.49 TPH ≈ 11.8 tonnes/day.","explain":"Direct-method questions in BEE papers often hide the efficiency inside the evaporation ratio. 'Relative' improvement means multiply by 1.02, not add 2 percentage points. The useful heat output stays the same, so coal varies inversely with efficiency.","steps":[{"prompt":"Heat added per kg of steam","answer":"790 − 130 = 660 kcal/kg","explain":"Feed-water enthalpy ≈ its temperature in °C."},{"prompt":"Boiler efficiency from ER","answer":"η = 4.8 × 660 / 4000 = 0.792 → 79.2%","explain":"η = ER × (hg − hf)/GCV."},{"prompt":"Present coal consumption","answer":"120 / 4.8 = 25.0 TPH","explain":"Coal = steam / ER."},{"prompt":"Improved efficiency (2% relative)","answer":"79.2 × 1.02 = 80.78%","explain":"Relative improvement multiplies the existing value."},{"prompt":"New coal and daily saving","answer":"Coal = 120,000×660/(0.80784×4000) = 24,510 kg/h; saving = 490 kg/h × 24 ≈ 11.8 t/day","explain":"Same heat output divided by the new efficiency × GCV."}],"flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P4 N3(ii)","order":4012},
{"id":"p4-014","paper":4,"topic":"Boiler efficiency","kind":"problem","front":"Two boilers burn the same fuel oil and both run at an evaporation ratio of 13.5. The LP boiler makes saturated steam (enthalpy 665 kcal/kg) from 70 °C feed water at 80% efficiency. The HP boiler makes superheated steam (enthalpy 740 kcal/kg) from 110 °C feed water. Find the HP boiler efficiency.","back":"≈ 84.7%","explain":"At the same ER and fuel, efficiency is proportional to the heat added per kg of steam (hg − hf). So η_HP = η_LP × (740 − 110)/(665 − 70). You can also back-calculate the fuel GCV first, which gives the same answer.","steps":[{"prompt":"Back-calculate fuel GCV from the LP boiler","answer":"GCV = 13.5 × (665 − 70)/0.80 = 10,041 kcal/kg","explain":"η = ER(hg − hf)/GCV, rearranged."},{"prompt":"Heat added per kg of steam in HP boiler","answer":"740 − 110 = 630 kcal/kg","explain":"hg − hf."},{"prompt":"HP boiler efficiency","answer":"η = 13.5 × 630 / 10,041 = 0.847 → 84.7%","explain":"Same ER, so efficiency rises with heat added per kg."}],"flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P4 L1","order":4013},
{"id":"p4-015","paper":4,"topic":"Boiler efficiency","kind":"problem","front":"Flue gas from a boiler has 3000 ppm CO and 13% CO₂. The fuel has 40 kg of carbon per 100 kg and a GCV of 4500 kcal/kg. Estimate the % heat loss due to incomplete combustion of carbon to CO.","back":"≈ 51 kcal/kg of fuel ≈ 1.13% loss","explain":"The partial-combustion loss uses the share of carbon that ends up as CO, times 5654 kcal per kg of such carbon. Convert ppm to % by dividing by 10,000. CO losses are usually small but signal poor burner tuning or too little air.","steps":[{"prompt":"Convert CO to %","answer":"3000 ppm / 10,000 = 0.3%","explain":"1% = 10,000 ppm."},{"prompt":"Heat lost per kg of fuel","answer":"[0.3 × 0.40/(0.3 + 13)] × 5654 = 51.0 kcal/kg","explain":"Loss = %CO × C/(%CO + %CO₂) × 5654."},{"prompt":"Percentage loss","answer":"51.0/4500 × 100 = 1.13%","explain":"Divide by GCV."}],"flash":false,"source":"BEE 2023 P4 L2","order":4014},
{"id":"p4-016","paper":4,"topic":"Boiler efficiency","kind":"problem","front":"An oil-fired boiler burns fuel with ultimate analysis C 84%, H 11.5%, S 3%, O 0.5%, N 0.5%, moisture 0.5%; GCV 10,200 kcal/kg. Flue gas O₂ = 3%, flue gas temperature 210 °C, ambient 30 °C. Take Cp of flue gas 0.24 and of superheated vapour 0.45 kcal/kg°C, humidity 0.025 kg/kg dry air, and radiation plus unaccounted loss 1.5%. Find the boiler efficiency by the indirect method.","back":"Efficiency ≈ 84.6% (L1 6.83%, L2 6.75%, L3 0.03%, L4 0.32%, others 1.5%)","explain":"This is the standard BEE indirect-method sequence: theoretical air → excess air → actual air → dry flue gas → each loss → 100 − Σlosses. For oil the hydrogen loss is nearly as large as the dry-flue-gas loss, because H is high. Both past papers (pet coke 2023, rice husk 2024) follow exactly these steps.","steps":[{"prompt":"Theoretical air","answer":"[11.6×84 + 34.8×(11.5 − 0.5/8) + 4.35×3]/100 = 13.85 kg/kg fuel","explain":"Stoichiometric air formula."},{"prompt":"Excess air and actual air","answer":"EA = 3/(21−3) = 16.7%; AAS = 13.85 × 1.167 = 16.16 kg/kg","explain":"EA% = O₂/(21 − O₂)."},{"prompt":"Mass of dry flue gas","answer":"0.84×44/12 + 0.03×64/32 + 0.005 + 0.77×16.16 + 0.23×(16.16−13.85) = 16.12 kg/kg","explain":"CO₂ + SO₂ + fuel N₂ + air N₂ + excess O₂."},{"prompt":"Dry flue gas loss L1","answer":"16.12 × 0.24 × 180/10,200 × 100 = 6.83%","explain":"m × Cp × ΔT / GCV."},{"prompt":"Moisture-related losses","answer":"L2 = 9×0.115×(584 + 0.45×180)/10,200 = 6.75%; L3 = 0.005×665/10,200 = 0.03%; L4 = 16.16×0.025×0.45×180/10,200 = 0.32%","explain":"Latent + superheat for H₂ and fuel moisture; sensible only for air humidity."},{"prompt":"Efficiency","answer":"100 − (6.83 + 6.75 + 0.03 + 0.32 + 1.5) = 84.6%","explain":"Indirect method: 100 − Σ losses."}],"flash":false,"source":"BEE 2023 P4 N3","order":4015},
{"id":"p4-017","paper":4,"topic":"Furnaces","kind":"fact","front":"How is the direct efficiency of a reheating furnace calculated?","back":"η = Heat taken up by stock / Heat in fuel = [m × Cp × (T_final − T_initial)] / (fuel × GCV) × 100.","explain":"Use the stock's mean specific heat (about 0.12–0.13 kcal/kg°C for steel billets). For oil, convert litres to kg with density first. Specific fuel consumption (litres or kg per tonne) is the everyday KPI derived from the same data.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":4016},
{"id":"p4-018","paper":4,"topic":"Furnaces","kind":"fact","front":"Should the heating zone of a reheating furnace be kept at a slight negative or slight positive pressure?","back":"Slight positive pressure, so cold air is not drawn in through doors and openings.","explain":"Negative pressure lets in cold air, which cools the stock surface, causes uneven heating and scaling, and wastes fuel. Too much positive pressure pushes flames and hot gas out of the openings. BEE 2023 T/F: 'negative pressure for uniform heating' is False.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2023 P4 Q1","order":4017},
{"id":"p4-019","paper":4,"topic":"Furnaces","kind":"problem","front":"An oil-fired billet reheating furnace uses 800 litres/h of furnace oil (density 0.93 kg/l, GCV 10,200 kcal/kg) to heat 20 TPH of billets from 30 °C to 1200 °C (mean Cp 0.13 kcal/kg°C). Oil costs ₹55/kg. An electric furnace of 92% efficiency is proposed, with power at ₹9/kWh. Find the oil furnace efficiency and the energy cost per tonne in both cases.","back":"Oil furnace η ≈ 40.1%, ₹2046/t; electric ≈ 192 kWh/t, ₹1730/t, so the electric furnace is cheaper.","explain":"Heat to stock is the same in both cases. The oil furnace wastes about 60% in flue gas and openings, while the electric furnace wastes only 8%. The decision depends on tariff vs fuel price, so compare cost per tonne, not just efficiency. Use 860 kcal = 1 kWh.","steps":[{"prompt":"Heat absorbed by billets","answer":"20,000 × 0.13 × (1200 − 30) = 30.42 lakh kcal/h","explain":"m Cp ΔT."},{"prompt":"Heat input from oil","answer":"800 × 0.93 = 744 kg/h; × 10,200 = 75.89 lakh kcal/h","explain":"Litres → kg via density."},{"prompt":"Oil furnace efficiency and cost/t","answer":"η = 30.42/75.89 = 40.1%; SEC = 744/20 = 37.2 kg/t → ₹2046/t","explain":"Efficiency = output/input."},{"prompt":"Electric furnace energy","answer":"30.42 lakh/860/0.92 = 3845 kWh/h → 192.2 kWh/t → ₹1730/t","explain":"1 kWh = 860 kcal; divide by furnace efficiency."}],"flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P4 L2","order":4018},
{"id":"p4-020","paper":4,"topic":"Heat exchangers","kind":"fact","front":"What is the formula for the log mean temperature difference (LMTD)?","back":"LMTD = (ΔT1 − ΔT2) / ln(ΔT1/ΔT2), with ΔT1, ΔT2 the terminal temperature differences. Then Q = U × A × LMTD.","explain":"For counterflow, ΔT1 = Th,in − Tc,out and ΔT2 = Th,out − Tc,in. For parallel flow, use the inlet-to-inlet and outlet-to-outlet differences. Counterflow gives a higher LMTD and so needs less area for the same duty. If ΔT1 = ΔT2, LMTD equals that value.","flash":true,"source":"syllabus","order":4019},
{"id":"p4-021","paper":4,"topic":"Heat exchangers","kind":"fact","front":"How is heat exchanger effectiveness defined?","back":"ε = actual heat transferred / maximum possible = C(ΔT of either stream) / [C_min × (Th,in − Tc,in)].","explain":"C = m × Cp is the heat-capacity rate. The fluid with the smaller C (C_min) could at best change temperature by the full inlet difference. A simple form is ε = (Tc,out − Tc,in)/(Th,in − Tc,in) when the cold fluid is C_min. Falling effectiveness at the same flows points to fouling.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":4020},
{"id":"p4-022","paper":4,"topic":"Heat exchangers","kind":"fact","front":"Does the overall heat-transfer coefficient (U) of a shell-and-tube exchanger fall only because the fluid temperatures change?","back":"No. U falls mainly because of fouling (scale, deposits) and changes in flow velocity, not just temperature changes.","explain":"1/U = sum of film resistances + wall resistance + fouling resistances. Fouling factors build up with time, which is why U is trended to plan cleaning. BEE 2024 T/F: False.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P4 Q9","order":4021},
{"id":"p4-023","paper":4,"topic":"Heat exchangers","kind":"problem","front":"In a counterflow plate heat exchanger, process water (150 m³/h) is cooled from 21 °C to 15 °C by chilled water (180 m³/h) entering at 7 °C and leaving at 12 °C. U = 2.5 kW/m²°C, Cp = 4.18 kJ/kg°C. Find the heat duty, LMTD, required area and effectiveness.","back":"Q ≈ 1045 kW; LMTD ≈ 8.49 °C; A ≈ 49.2 m²; ε ≈ 42.9%","explain":"Check the energy balance first: 150 × 6 = 180 × 5, so both sides carry the same duty. Process water has the smaller heat-capacity rate, so it is C_min and its ΔT goes in the effectiveness numerator.","steps":[{"prompt":"Heat duty","answer":"(150 × 1000/3600) × 4.18 × (21 − 15) = 1045 kW","explain":"Q = m Cp ΔT; 1 m³ water ≈ 1000 kg."},{"prompt":"Terminal differences (counterflow)","answer":"ΔT1 = 21 − 12 = 9 °C; ΔT2 = 15 − 7 = 8 °C","explain":"Hot in vs cold out; hot out vs cold in."},{"prompt":"LMTD","answer":"(9 − 8)/ln(9/8) = 8.49 °C","explain":"LMTD formula."},{"prompt":"Area","answer":"A = 1045/(2.5 × 8.49) = 49.2 m²","explain":"Q = U A LMTD."},{"prompt":"Effectiveness","answer":"ε = (21 − 15)/(21 − 7) = 6/14 = 42.9%","explain":"Hot side is C_min (150 < 180 m³/h)."}],"flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P4 N1","order":4022},
{"id":"p4-024","paper":4,"topic":"Electric motors","kind":"fact","front":"How is three-phase input power calculated from measured values?","back":"P (kW) = √3 × V_L × I × cosφ / 1000.","explain":"V_L is line voltage, I is line current, cosφ is power factor. A power analyser gives kW directly. Motor output = input × efficiency (or input − losses), and this output is the pump/fan shaft power.","flash":true,"source":"syllabus","order":4023},
{"id":"p4-025","paper":4,"topic":"Electric motors","kind":"fact","front":"How are synchronous speed and slip defined, and how does slip vary with load?","back":"Ns = 120f/P; slip = (Ns − N)/Ns. Slip rises roughly in proportion to load, so higher slip means higher motor loading.","explain":"For a 4-pole 50 Hz motor, Ns = 1500 rpm. Load can be estimated as (Ns − N_measured)/(Ns − N_rated) × 100%, which is handy when power cannot be measured. Slip is also affected by voltage, so treat this as an estimate. BEE 2023 T/F: 'higher slip, higher loading' is True.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2023 P4 Q3","order":4024},
{"id":"p4-026","paper":4,"topic":"Electric motors","kind":"fact","front":"Is the power factor of an induction motor high when it runs at rated voltage and frequency with no shaft load?","back":"No. At no load the power factor is very low (often 0.1–0.3), because the current is mostly magnetising (reactive) current.","explain":"Magnetising kVAr stays almost constant while the active power at no load only covers core and friction losses. This is why under-loaded motors drag down plant power factor. BEE 2023 T/F: False.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2023 P4 Q6","order":4025},
{"id":"p4-027","paper":4,"topic":"Electric motors","kind":"fact","front":"How is the percentage loading of a motor estimated from input power?","back":"Load % = (Measured input kW × motor efficiency) / Rated output kW × 100.","explain":"Rated (nameplate) kW is shaft output, so input must be multiplied by efficiency before comparing. Motors loaded below about 50% run at lower efficiency and power factor, which makes them candidates for downsizing or star-delta / VFD operation.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":4026},
{"id":"p4-028","paper":4,"topic":"Electric motors","kind":"problem","front":"A 75 kW, 4-pole, 50 Hz motor has a nameplate speed of 1475 rpm and a full-load efficiency of 93%. In operation it runs at 1484 rpm and draws 88 A at 415 V with a PF of 0.82. Estimate the loading by the slip method and by the input-power method.","back":"Slip method ≈ 64%; input-power method ≈ 64.3% (input ≈ 51.9 kW)","explain":"The two methods agree closely here, which is a good check on the measurements. Using full-load efficiency at 64% load is a slight approximation, since efficiency changes little between 50% and 100% load.","steps":[{"prompt":"Synchronous speed","answer":"Ns = 120 × 50/4 = 1500 rpm","explain":"Ns = 120f/P."},{"prompt":"Load by slip","answer":"(1500 − 1484)/(1500 − 1475) = 16/25 = 64%","explain":"Slip is roughly proportional to load."},{"prompt":"Measured input power","answer":"√3 × 415 × 88 × 0.82 / 1000 = 51.87 kW","explain":"Three-phase power."},{"prompt":"Load by input power","answer":"51.87 × 0.93/75 = 64.3%","explain":"Output = input × η; compare with rated output."}],"flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":4027},
{"id":"p4-029","paper":4,"topic":"Pumps","kind":"fact","front":"What is the formula for pump hydraulic power?","back":"P_h (kW) = Q (m³/s) × H (m) × ρ (kg/m³) × g / 1000.","explain":"H is total head: discharge head minus suction head, both measured, plus any velocity-head correction. With water, 1 kg/cm² ≈ 10 m. Pump efficiency = P_h / shaft power, and overall (pump-set) efficiency = P_h / motor input.","flash":true,"source":"syllabus","order":4028},
{"id":"p4-030","paper":4,"topic":"Pumps","kind":"fact","front":"How do pump efficiency and overall pump-set efficiency differ?","back":"Pump efficiency = hydraulic power / shaft input (motor input × motor efficiency). Overall efficiency = hydraulic power / motor electrical input.","explain":"Motor output can also be found as motor input minus motor losses. Exams often give motor losses in kW instead of efficiency. Always say which efficiency you are quoting.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P4 L1","order":4029},
{"id":"p4-031","paper":4,"topic":"Pumps & fans","kind":"fact","front":"State the affinity laws for centrifugal pumps and fans.","back":"Flow ∝ N, Head (pressure) ∝ N², Power ∝ N³ (speed N; similar laws hold for impeller diameter).","explain":"A 20% speed cut can lower power by about 49% (0.8³ = 0.512). This cube law is why VFDs save so much on variable-flow fans and pumps. The laws hold strictly only for a system curve with no static head.","flash":true,"source":"syllabus","order":4030},
{"id":"p4-032","paper":4,"topic":"Pumps","kind":"fact","front":"Do the affinity laws apply to a pump on a VFD running at low speed against a high static head?","back":"No. With a large static head the operating point moves along the system curve, so flow and power do not follow the N, N², N³ ratios.","explain":"Affinity laws relate similar operating points on a system curve that passes through the origin (friction-only). With high static head, a small speed reduction can cut flow sharply and push the pump into a low-efficiency region. BEE 2024 T/F: True.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P4 Q2","order":4031},
{"id":"p4-033","paper":4,"topic":"Pumps","kind":"fact","front":"For the same head and flow, does a pump use more power on water or on kerosene?","back":"More power on water. Hydraulic power ∝ density, and kerosene (≈800 kg/m³) is lighter than water.","explain":"P = ρ g Q H. Head in metres of liquid is the same, but the mass lifted per second is smaller for kerosene. BEE 2023 T/F: 'water consumes less' is False.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2023 P4 Q7","order":4032},
{"id":"p4-034","paper":4,"topic":"Pumps","kind":"fact","front":"How does pipe friction head depend on velocity and diameter?","back":"h_f = 4 f L v² / (2 g D) (Fanning f). For a fixed flow, h_f ∝ 1/D⁵, so a slightly larger pipe cuts friction power sharply.","explain":"Because v = Q/A ∝ 1/D², v²/D ∝ 1/D⁵. BEE compared two 500 mm lines with one 750 mm line for 1000 l/s: the larger single line had about half the friction head. Total head = static head + friction head.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2023 P4 N2","order":4033},
{"id":"p4-035","paper":4,"topic":"Pumps","kind":"problem","front":"A cooling water pump delivers 1200 m³/h. Suction gauge reads 0.5 kg/cm² and discharge gauge reads 4.5 kg/cm² (gauges at the same level). The motor draws 190 kW and its efficiency is 94%. Find the hydraulic power, pump efficiency and overall pump-set efficiency.","back":"Hydraulic power ≈ 130.8 kW; pump η ≈ 73.2%; overall η ≈ 68.8%","explain":"The pump adds the difference between discharge and suction pressure. 1 kg/cm² ≈ 10 m of water. Shaft power is motor output, not motor input.","steps":[{"prompt":"Total head","answer":"(4.5 − 0.5) × 10 = 40 m","explain":"1 kg/cm² ≈ 10 m water column."},{"prompt":"Hydraulic power","answer":"(1200/3600) × 1000 × 9.81 × 40/1000 = 130.8 kW","explain":"P = Q ρ g H."},{"prompt":"Shaft power","answer":"190 × 0.94 = 178.6 kW","explain":"Motor output = input × motor efficiency."},{"prompt":"Pump and overall efficiency","answer":"Pump η = 130.8/178.6 = 73.2%; overall = 130.8/190 = 68.8%","explain":"Overall uses electrical input."}],"flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P4 L1","order":4034},
{"id":"p4-036","paper":4,"topic":"Pumps","kind":"problem","front":"0.4 m³/s of water is to be pumped through a 2.5 km pipeline against a static head of 20 m. Compare a 450 mm pipe with a 600 mm pipe (Fanning friction factor 0.005, h_f = 4fLv²/2gD). Pump efficiency 80%, motor 94%, 8000 h/yr, ₹8/kWh. Find the annual energy saving from the larger pipe.","back":"450 mm: ≈ 291 kW; 600 mm: ≈ 149 kW; saving ≈ 11.4 lakh kWh/yr ≈ ₹91 lakh/yr","explain":"Friction head falls from about 35.8 m to 8.5 m because it varies with 1/D⁵. Static head is the same in both cases. The extra capital cost of the larger pipe is usually repaid quickly when flow is large and runs continuously.","steps":[{"prompt":"Velocities","answer":"A₄₅₀ = 0.159 m² → v = 2.52 m/s; A₆₀₀ = 0.283 m² → v = 1.41 m/s","explain":"v = Q/A, A = πD²/4."},{"prompt":"Friction heads","answer":"450 mm: 4×0.005×2500×2.515²/(2×9.81×0.45) = 35.8 m; 600 mm: 8.5 m","explain":"Fanning-form Darcy equation."},{"prompt":"Total heads","answer":"450 mm: 55.8 m; 600 mm: 28.5 m","explain":"Static 20 m + friction."},{"prompt":"Motor input power","answer":"450 mm: 0.4×55.8×9.81/(0.8×0.94) = 291.3 kW; 600 mm: 148.7 kW","explain":"Hydraulic power / (η_pump × η_motor)."},{"prompt":"Annual saving","answer":"(291.3 − 148.7) × 8000 = 11.41 lakh kWh → ₹91.2 lakh","explain":"kW × hours × tariff."}],"flash":false,"source":"BEE 2023 P4 N2","order":4035},
{"id":"p4-037","paper":4,"topic":"Fans & blowers","kind":"fact","front":"What is the formula for fan air power when pressure is in mmWC?","back":"Air power (kW) = Q (m³/s) × ΔP (mmWC) / 102. (With Pa: Q × ΔP / 1000.)","explain":"1 mmWC = 9.81 Pa, so dividing by 102 converts mmWC × m³/s to kW. Use the static pressure rise for static efficiency, or the total pressure rise for total (mechanical) efficiency. Fan efficiency = air power / shaft power (motor input × motor efficiency).","flash":true,"source":"syllabus","order":4036},
{"id":"p4-038","paper":4,"topic":"Fans & blowers","kind":"fact","front":"How is gas velocity in a duct found from a Pitot-tube reading?","back":"v (m/s) = Cp × √(2 × 9.81 × Δp × γ) / γ, with Δp = velocity pressure in mmWC, γ = gas density = 1.293 × 273/(273 + t) kg/m³, Cp = Pitot constant.","explain":"The density correction matters for hot gases: air at 60 °C is about 1.06 kg/m³ instead of 1.293. Velocity is not uniform across a duct, so readings are taken at several traverse points and averaged (BEE 2024 T/F: 'velocity is uniform' is False). Flow = average velocity × duct area.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P4 Q1","order":4037},
{"id":"p4-039","paper":4,"topic":"Fans & blowers","kind":"fact","front":"Is a roof exhaust fan on an industrial shed an axial or a centrifugal fan?","back":"Axial flow. It moves large volumes at very low pressure, with air moving parallel to the shaft.","explain":"Axial fans (propeller, tube-axial, vane-axial) suit high-flow, low-pressure duties such as roof exhausters, cooling towers and ventilation. Centrifugal fans (forward-curved, backward-curved, radial) suit higher pressures, such as ID/FD fans. BEE 2023 T/F: True.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2023 P4 Q4","order":4038}
]$cards$::jsonb);
